      "text": "To determine the least whole number of hours greater than \\(4\\) hours that is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number of hours on a 12-hour clock, we need to find an integer \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\) is \"clock equivalent\" to \\( m = n^2 \\).\n\nThis means that \\( n \\) should be the same when viewed as \\( m \\) modulo 12. Therefore, we need to find the smallest \\( n > 4 \\) such that:\n\\[ n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nWe start by checking the values of \\( n \\) one by one, starting from \\( n = 5 \\):\n\n1. For \\( n = 5 \\):\n   \\[ n^2 = 5^2 = 25 \\]\n   \\[ 25 \\mod 12 = 25 - 2 \\cdot 12 = 25 - 24 = 1 \\]\n   So, \\( 5 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\), which means \\( 5 \\) is not \"clock equivalent\" to \\( 25 \\).\n\n2. For \\( n = 6 \\):\n   \\[ n^2 = 6^2 = 36 \\]\n   \\[ 36 \\mod 12 = 36 - 3 \\cdot 12 = 36 - 36 = 0 \\]\n   So, \\( 6 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\), which means \\( 6 \\) is not \"clock equivalent\" to \\( 36 \\).\n\n3. For \\( n = 7 \\):\n   \\[ n^2 = 7^2 = 49 \\]\n   \\[ 49 \\mod 12 = 49 - 4 \\cdot 12 = 49 - 48 = 1 \\]\n   So, \\( 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{12} \\), which means \\( 7 \\) is not \"clock equivalent\" to \\( 49 \\).\n\n4. For \\( n = 8 \\):\n   \\[ n^2 = 8^2 = 64 \\]\n   \\[ 64 \\mod 12 = 64 - 5 \\cdot 12 = 64 - 60 = 4 \\]\n   So, \\( 8 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{12} \\), which means \\( 8 \\) is not \"clock equivalent\" to \\( 64 \\).\n\n5. For \\( n = 9 \\):\n   \\[ n^2 = 9^2 = 81 \\]\n   \\[ 81 \\mod 12 = 81 - 6 \\cdot 12 = 81 - 72 = 9 \\]\n   So, \\( 9 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{12} \\), which means \\( 9 \\) is \"clock equivalent\" to \\( 81 \\).\n\nSince \\( 9 \\) is the smallest whole number greater than \\( 4 \\) that is \"clock equivalent\" to its square number \\( 81 \\) on a 12-hour clock, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
