    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the smallest whole number of hours \\( n \\) greater than 4 hours such that the elapsed time of \\( n \\) hours is \"clock equivalent\" to its square, i.e., \\( n \\) and \\( n^2 \\) have the same remainder when divided by 12.\n\nThis can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[ n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ n^2 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\nor\n\\[ n(n-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nThis means that \\( n(n-1) \\) must be divisible by 12. Since \\( n \\) and \\( n-1 \\) are consecutive integers, one of them must be divisible by 2 and one must be divisible by 6. Therefore, \\( n \\) can be either a multiple of 6 or one more than a multiple of 6.\n\nWe start by checking the multiples of 6 greater than 4:\n\\[ 6 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ 12 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ 18 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ 24 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\nThe smallest multiple of 6 that satisfies the condition is 6. Now, we check if 6 and \\( 6^2 = 36 \\) have the same remainder when divided by 12:\n\\[ 6 \\div 12 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\]\n\\[ 36 \\div 12 = 3 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\]\n\nSince 6 and 36 have the same remainder when divided by 12, the smallest whole number of hours greater than 4 hours that is \"clock equivalent\" to its square is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
